  "body": {
    "id": "prop:upper-endpoint-attainment",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every observed law \\(p=p(P_0)\\) induced by some \\(P_0\\in\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{ASC}}\\) and every fixed \\(t\\in\\mathcal T\\), the upper construction returns one law \\(P_t^+(p)\\in\\mathcal F(p)\\) satisfying \\(Q_{1,P_t^+(p)}(t)-Q_{0,P_t^+(p)}(t)=\\overline\\Delta(t;p)\\). Both marginal quantiles in this equality are generated by the same compatible law.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "p",
      "P",
      "Mlaw",
      "calT",
      "PplusT",
      "Ffiber",
      "Qtrue",
      "DeltaUp"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:causal-model",
      "def:compatible-law-fiber",
      "def:finite-fiber-polytope",
      "prop:fiber-polytope-equivalence",
      "def:upper-endpoint-witness"
    ],
    "justification": "Separate same-law attainment makes the upper coordinate endpoint genuinely sharp rather than a difference of independently optimized marginal bounds.",
    "gap": "The marginal trimming formulas do not by themselves establish attainment of their difference within one latent completion of the noncompliance-and-selection model.",
    "consumer": "Each upper-endpoint sign label in the Job Corps assertion list has an auditable compatible-law witness.",
    "proof_tex": "The compactness and quantile characterization (4) in the lower-endpoint proof apply unchanged. The positive common-slack program for \\((j_0,j_1)\\) is feasible exactly when one \\(\\lambda\\in\\Xi(p)\\) realizes both grid quantiles. Selecting the feasible pair with largest \\(y_{j_1}-y_{j_0}\\), and then its lexicographically least positive-slack basic maximizer, produces one \\(P_t^+(p)\\in\\mathcal F(p)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n Q_{1,P_t^+(p)}(t)-Q_{0,P_t^+(p)}(t)\n =\\max_{\\lambda\\in\\Xi(p)}\n \\{Q_{1,P_\\lambda}(t)-Q_{0,P_\\lambda}(t)\\}\n =\\overline\\Delta(t;p).\n\\]\nThe maximum exists because this objective has finite range on the nonempty compact fiber.",
